    "cefr_range": "B2-C1",
    "reading_text": "\\nIn the late 19th century, Sir William Crookes was a prominent British chemist and physicist. He is best known for his work on X-rays, which he discovered in 1895 alongside Wilhelm Röntgen. However, his contributions to the field of electricity were equally important. Crookes's research led to the development of the cathode ray tube (CRT), a device that uses X-rays or other forms of radiation to illuminate a screen, making it possible for people to see images on televisions and computer monitors. \\n\\nDespite his accomplishments, Crookes was not particularly interested in applying his discoveries to practical uses. He believed that science should be pursued purely for its own sake and that any commercial applications would undermine the integrity of research. This stance led him to decline numerous offers from industrialists who wanted to use his technology in their businesses.",
